<clickhouse>
    <!-- DEVELOPMENT ONLY. Mounted by docker-compose.yaml, not by
         compose.infra.yaml, so no deployment target inherits it.

         `old_parts_lifetime` is how long a part superseded by a merge or a
         mutation is kept before deletion. Its purpose is recovery from an
         unclean shutdown: a part deleted too eagerly cannot be brought back.
         480 seconds is the right answer for a store holding customer data.

         It is the wrong answer for a laptop running the gate. The live suites
         exercise the product's own tenant purge (ADR-014), which is an
         `ALTER DELETE` — and a tenant is not a partition, so each one rewrites
         every part of every telemetry table. Measured across two consecutive
         `just check` runs: 16,253 superseded parts, with jemalloc holding
         3.17 GiB against a 4 GiB server cap — live allocation, not allocator
         retention, so nothing returns it until the parts expire eight minutes
         later. The second gate run then fails with MEMORY_LIMIT_EXCEEDED in
         suites that have nothing to do with purging.

         Thirty seconds is long enough to survive the crash window that matters
         here (there is none: the data is a demo dataset regenerated by
         `just load`) and short enough that the parts a purge supersedes are
         gone before the next suite needs the memory. -->
    <merge_tree>
        <old_parts_lifetime>30</old_parts_lifetime>
    </merge_tree>
</clickhouse>
